  <section id="configuration-environment" class="configuration-section">
    <div class="configuration-heading">
      <span>01</span>
      <div>
        <label>ENVIRONMENT</label>
        <h2>Environment Variables</h2>
        <p>
          AVSS Lite validates environment variables with a Zod schema
          through validateEnv().
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="configuration-table">
      <div class="configuration-table-row configuration-table-header">
        <span>Variable</span><span>Validation</span><span>Required</span>
      </div>
      <div class="configuration-table-row">
        <code>NODE_ENV</code><span>development, production, test</span><b>Default: development</b>
      </div>
      <div class="configuration-table-row">
        <code>DATABASE_URL</code><span>Non-empty string</span><b>Yes</b>
      </div>
      <div class="configuration-table-row">
        <code>BETTER_AUTH_SECRET</code><span>Minimum 32 characters</span><b>Yes</b>
      </div>
      <div class="configuration-table-row">
        <code>BETTER_AUTH_URL</code><span>Valid URL</span><b>Yes</b>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="configuration-subtitle">Example .env</h3>
    <CodeBlock language="bash" :code="`NODE_ENV=development
DATABASE_URL=&quot;postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/mydb&quot;
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=&quot;replace-with-at-least-32-characters&quot;
BETTER_AUTH_URL=&quot;http://localhost:3000&quot;`" />

    <h3 class="configuration-subtitle">Validate environment</h3>
    <CodeBlock language="typescript" :code="`import { validateEnv } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const env = validateEnv();

console.log(env.NODE_ENV);
console.log(env.DATABASE_URL);
console.log(env.BETTER_AUTH_URL);`" />

    <p class="configuration-note">
      The schema uses Zod parsing. Invalid or missing required values
      cause validation to throw instead of returning an invalid Env object.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="configuration-security" class="configuration-section">
    <div class="configuration-heading">
      <span>02</span>
      <div>
        <label>SECURITY FRAMEWORK</label>
        <h2>Security Configuration</h2>
        <p>
          Pass a SecurityConfig object to createSecurity().
          Every top-level module configuration is optional.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock language="typescript" :code="`import { createSecurity } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const security = createSecurity({
  csrf: {},
  xss: {},
  headers: {},
  cors: {},
  cookies: {},
  proxy: {},
  rateLimit: {},
});`" />

    <div class="configuration-module-grid">
      <article><strong>csrf</strong><span>CSRF protection configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>xss</strong><span>XSS sanitization configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>headers</strong><span>Security headers configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>cors</strong><span>Cross-origin resource sharing configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>cookies</strong><span>Cookie handling configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>proxy</strong><span>Trusted proxy configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>rateLimit</strong><span>Rate limiting configuration</span></article>
    </div>

    <p class="configuration-note">
      The example above illustrates the available configuration structure.
      Empty objects do not customize module behavior. Refer to each module's
      configuration type for its supported properties and defaults.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="configuration-initialization" class="configuration-section">
    <div class="configuration-heading">
      <span>03</span>
      <div>
        <label>INITIALIZATION</label>
        <h2>Security Initialization</h2>
        <p>
          SecurityFramework receives the configuration in its constructor.
          The createSecurity() factory provides the package entry point.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock language="typescript" :code="`import { createSecurity } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const security = createSecurity();

console.log(security.csrf);
console.log(security.xss);
console.log(security.headers);
console.log(security.cors);
console.log(security.cookies);
console.log(security.proxy);
console.log(security.rateLimit);`" />

    <div class="configuration-callout">
      <strong>Important</strong>
      <span>
        createSecurity(config) calls setSecurityConfig(config).
        Initialize it with the intended configuration before using
        the security utilities.
      </span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="configuration-database" class="configuration-section">
    <div class="configuration-heading">
      <span>04</span>
      <div>
        <label>DATABASE</label>
        <h2>Prisma Client</h2>
        <p>
          AVSS Lite exposes a reusable PrismaClient singleton.
          In non-production environments, the instance is stored globally.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock language="typescript" :code="`import { prisma } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

// Reuse the exported Prisma client
const result = await prisma.$queryRaw\`SELECT 1\`;

console.log(result);`" />

    <p class="configuration-note">
      The Prisma client is initialized by the package.
      Its connection configuration is handled through the Prisma
      datasource and environment setup. DATABASE_URL is validated
      by the environment schema.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="configuration-next" class="configuration-section configuration-next">
    <span class="configuration-eyebrow">NEXT STEP</span>
    <h2>Explore security principles.</h2>
    <p>Understand the security modules and the protection concepts behind AVSS Lite.</p>
    <button class="primary-button" type="button"
      @click="selectPage('Security Principles')">
      Explore Security Principles <span>→</span>
    </button>
  </section>

</div>
<div v-else-if="activePage === 'Authentication'" class="authentication-page">
  <!-- AVSS AUTHENTICATION PAGE -->
  <section id="authentication-overview" class="auth-hero">
    <span class="auth-eyebrow">IDENTITY & ACCESS</span>
    <h1>Authentication</h1>
    <p class="auth-lead">
      Integrate application authentication with AVSS Lite security utilities.
      Build your authentication layer while keeping session handling and
      request protection within your application's control.
    </p>
    <div class="auth-status">
      <span class="auth-status-dot"></span>
      Security toolkit integration
    </div>
    </section>

  <section id="authentication-boundary" class="auth-section">
    <div class="auth-section-heading">
      <span>01</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Authentication Architecture</h2>
        <p>Understand the responsibility of AVSS Lite in an authentication system.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="auth-notice">
      <strong>Important: AVSS Lite is not an authentication provider.</strong>
      <p>
        Version 1.0.2 does not expose built-in sign-in, sign-up, sign-out,
        OAuth authorization, or password authentication methods. Your
        application must provide its own authentication provider and
        authentication flow.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="auth-grid">
      <article class="auth-card">
        <span class="auth-card-icon">01</span>
        <h3>Application Authentication</h3>
        <p>
          Your application or chosen authentication provider manages identity,
          credentials, sign-in, and account registration.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="auth-card">
        <span class="auth-card-icon">02</span>
        <h3>AVSS Lite Security</h3>
        <p>
          AVSS Lite provides security utilities such as CSRF protection,
          cookie utilities, rate limiting, and security headers.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="auth-card">
        <span class="auth-card-icon">03</span>
        <h3>Session Error Handling</h3>
        <p>
          The package provides SessionExpiredError to represent an expired
          session with an HTTP 401 status.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="authentication-session" class="auth-section">
    <div class="auth-section-heading">
      <span>02</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Session Handling</h2>
        <p>Session lifecycle management remains part of your application.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="auth-description">
      Your authentication provider is responsible for creating, validating,
      refreshing, and terminating sessions. AVSS Lite exposes cookie utilities
      through its security framework and provides a dedicated expired-session
      error.
    </p>
    <div class="auth-code-card">
      <div class="auth-code-heading">
        <span>Session error</span>
        <span>TypeScript</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code>import { SessionExpiredError } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

// Use when the application detects an expired session.
throw new SessionExpiredError();

// Optional custom message:
throw new SessionExpiredError('Your session has expired');</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="auth-inline-info">
      <strong>HTTP status:</strong> 401
      <span>·</span>
      <strong>Error code:</strong> SESSION_EXPIRED
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="authentication-security" class="auth-section">
    <div class="auth-section-heading">
      <span>03</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Authentication Security</h2>
        <p>Use AVSS Lite security utilities around protected application routes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="auth-code-card">
      <div class="auth-code-heading">
        <span>Initialize security utilities</span>
        <span>TypeScript</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code>import { createSecurity } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const security = createSecurity();

// Available security utilities:
security.csrf.generateToken;
security.csrf.validateToken;
security.csrf.middleware;

security.cookies.parse;
security.cookies.serialize;

security.rateLimit.check;
security.headers.builder;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="auth-description">
      These utilities are available for integration into your application's
      request and session security flow. Refer to the Security Principles
      and API Reference pages for their individual responsibilities.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="authentication-responsibilities" class="auth-section">
    <div class="auth-section-heading">
      <span>04</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Integration Responsibilities</h2>
        <p>Keep authentication ownership explicit in your application architecture.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="auth-responsibility-list">
      <div><span>01</span><p><strong>Identity provider</strong> — Select and configure the authentication provider used by your application.</p></div>
      <div><span>02</span><p><strong>Credentials</strong> — Handle registration, login, password verification, and account recovery in your authentication layer.</p></div>
      <div><span>03</span><p><strong>Sessions</strong> — Define session creation, validation, expiration, and termination policies.</p></div>
      <div><span>04</span><p><strong>Request protection</strong> — Apply AVSS Lite security utilities where appropriate in your request lifecycle.</p></div>
      <div><span>05</span><p><strong>Authorization</strong> — Enforce permissions and access policies in your application.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="authentication-next" class="auth-next">
    <div>
      <span class="auth-eyebrow">CONTINUE LEARNING</span>
      <h2>Protect authentication credentials.</h2>
      <p>Continue to Password Security to explore the next security topic.</p>
    </div>
    <button @click="selectPage('Password Security')">
      Password Security <span>→</span>
    </button>
  </section>
</div>
<div v-else-if="activePage === 'Password Security'" class="password-security-page">
  <!-- AVSS PASSWORD SECURITY PAGE -->
  <section class="password-hero" id="password-overview">
    <span class="password-eyebrow">CREDENTIAL PROTECTION</span>
    <h1>Password Security</h1>
    <p>
      Understand the role of password protection in an application using
      AVSS Lite. Keep credential management within your authentication layer
      and use AVSS Lite for its available security utilities.
    </p>
    <span class="password-status">APPLICATION-LEVEL RESPONSIBILITY</span>
  </section>

  <section class="password-section" id="password-scope">
    <div class="password-heading">
      <span>01</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Package Capabilities</h2>
        <p>What AVSS Lite v1.0.2 provides — and what it does not.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="password-notice">
      <strong>No built-in password hashing or verification API</strong>
      <p>
        The current AVSS Lite source does not expose a dedicated password
        module, password hashing function, password comparison function,
        or password reset API. These responsibilities belong to the
        application's authentication provider or credential-management layer.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="password-capability-grid">
      <article>
        <span>AVAILABLE</span>
        <h3>Security Utilities</h3>
        <p>CSRF protection, cookie utilities, rate limiting, security headers, and other security components.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>AVAILABLE</span>
        <h3>Authentication Error</h3>
        <p>SessionExpiredError represents an expired session with HTTP status 401.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>NOT EXPOSED</span>
        <h3>Password API</h3>
        <p>No dedicated password hashing, comparison, or password-management API is exported by the current package.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="password-section" id="password-integration">
    <div class="password-heading">
      <span>02</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Credential Integration</h2>
        <p>Keep password operations in your chosen authentication layer.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="password-description">
      When building credential-based authentication, select an appropriate
      authentication provider or password-hashing implementation. AVSS Lite
      does not replace that component.
    </p>
    <div class="password-code-card">
      <div class="password-code-title">
        <span>AVSS Lite security integration</span>
        <span>TypeScript</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code>import { createSecurity } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const security = createSecurity();

// Available security utilities:
security.csrf.generateToken;
security.csrf.validateToken;
security.cookies.parse;
security.cookies.serialize;
security.rateLimit.check;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="password-tip">
      <strong>Integration boundary</strong>
      <p>
        The example initializes AVSS Lite security utilities. It does not
        hash, store, or verify passwords. Connect these utilities to the
        request lifecycle implemented by your application.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="password-section" id="password-responsibilities">
    <div class="password-heading">
      <span>03</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Application Responsibilities</h2>
        <p>Password lifecycle operations must be implemented outside this package.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="password-responsibility-list">
      <div><span>01</span><p><strong>Password hashing</strong> — Select and configure a dedicated password-hashing implementation.</p></div>
      <div><span>02</span><p><strong>Password verification</strong> — Verify submitted credentials through your authentication layer.</p></div>
      <div><span>03</span><p><strong>Credential storage</strong> — Define secure storage and access policies for credential data.</p></div>
      <div><span>04</span><p><strong>Password recovery</strong> — Implement account recovery and password reset flows in your application.</p></div>
      <div><span>05</span><p><strong>Request protection</strong> — Apply relevant AVSS Lite security utilities to protected requests.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="password-next">
    <div>
      <span class="password-eyebrow">CONTINUE LEARNING</span>
      <h2>Handle application errors consistently.</h2>
      <p>Explore the error classes and structured error responses provided by AVSS Lite.</p>
    </div>
    <button @click="selectPage('Error Handling')">Error Handling <span>→</span></button>
  </section>
</div>
<div v-else-if="activePage === 'Error Handling'" class="error-handling-page">
  <!-- AVSS ERROR HANDLING PAGE -->
  <section class="error-hero" id="error-overview">
    <span class="error-eyebrow">EXCEPTION MANAGEMENT</span>
    <h1>Error Handling</h1>
    <p>
      Handle application exceptions with AVSS Lite's structured error
      classes and centralized error response utility.
    </p>
    <span class="error-status">STRUCTURED ERROR RESPONSES</span>
  </section>

  <section class="error-section" id="error-architecture">
    <div class="error-heading">
      <span>01</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Error Architecture</h2>
        <p>A common base class provides consistent error metadata.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="error-code-card">
      <div class="error-code-title">
        <span>AVSSError</span>
        <span>TypeScript</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code>export class AVSSError extends Error {
  public readonly status: number;
  public readonly code: ErrorCode;
  public readonly timestamp: Date;

  constructor(
    message: string,
    status: number,
    code: ErrorCode
  ) {
    super(message);

    this.name = this.constructor.name;
    this.status = status;
    this.code = code;
    this.timestamp = new Date();
  }
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="error-metadata-grid">
      <article><strong>message</strong><p>Human-readable error description.</p></article>
      <article><strong>status</strong><p>HTTP status associated with the error.</p></article>
      <article><strong>code</strong><p>Machine-readable ErrorCode identifier.</p></article>
      <article><strong>timestamp</strong><p>Date and time when the error was created.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="error-section" id="error-types">
    <div class="error-heading">
      <span>02</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Built-in Error Classes</h2>
        <p>Specialized errors available from the package.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="error-table-wrap">
      <table class="error-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Class</th><th>HTTP Status</th><th>Error Code</th><th>Default Message</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>UnauthorizedError</td><td>401</td><td>UNAUTHORIZED</td><td>Unauthorized</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ForbiddenError</td><td>403</td><td>FORBIDDEN</td><td>Forbidden</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ValidationError</td><td>400</td><td>VALIDATION_ERROR</td><td>Validation Failed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SessionExpiredError</td><td>401</td><td>SESSION_EXPIRED</td><td>Session Expired</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InternalServerError</td><td>500</td><td>INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR</td><td>Internal Server Error</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="error-section" id="error-codes">
    <div class="error-heading">
      <span>03</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Error Codes</h2>
        <p>Use ErrorCode to identify supported error categories.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="error-code-card">
      <div class="error-code-title">
        <span>ErrorCode</span>
        <span>TypeScript</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code>export enum ErrorCode {
  UNAUTHORIZED = "UNAUTHORIZED",
  FORBIDDEN = "FORBIDDEN",
  VALIDATION_ERROR = "VALIDATION_ERROR",
  SESSION_EXPIRED = "SESSION_EXPIRED",
  INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR = "INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR",
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="error-section" id="error-handler">
    <div class="error-heading">
      <span>04</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Centralized Error Handler</h2>
        <p>Convert known and unknown exceptions into a consistent response structure.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="error-code-card">
      <div class="error-code-title">
        <span>Using errorHandler()</span>
        <span>TypeScript</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code>import {
  errorHandler,
  UnauthorizedError
} from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const response = errorHandler(
  new UnauthorizedError('Authentication required')
);

console.log(response);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="error-description">
      The errorHandler function accepts an unknown error. If it receives
      an AVSSError instance, it preserves its error metadata. Otherwise,
      it returns a generic InternalServerError response with HTTP status 500.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="error-section" id="error-response">
    <div class="error-heading">
      <span>05</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Error Response Structure</h2>
        <p>The handler returns a predictable ErrorResponse object.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="error-code-card">
      <div class="error-code-title">
        <span>Example response</span>
        <span>JSON</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code>{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "name": "UnauthorizedError",
    "message": "Authentication required",
    "status": 401,
    "code": "UNAUTHORIZED",
    "timestamp": "2026-11-05T08:00:00.000Z"
  }
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="error-tip">
      <strong>Timestamp format</strong>
      <p>The handler converts the Date value to an ISO 8601 string using toISOString(). The timestamp above is illustrative.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="error-section" id="error-serialization">
    <div class="error-heading">
      <span>06</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Error Serialization</h2>
        <p>AVSSError also provides a toJSON() method.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="error-code-card">
      <div class="error-code-title">
        <span>Serialize an error</span>
        <span>TypeScript</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code>import { ForbiddenError } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const error = new ForbiddenError('Access denied');

const serialized = error.toJSON();

console.log(serialized);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="error-description">
      The serialized object contains name, message, status, code, and
      timestamp. The timestamp is represented as an ISO 8601 string.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="error-next">
    <div>
      <span class="error-eyebrow">CONTINUE LEARNING</span>
      <h2>Explore the complete API.</h2>
      <p>Review the exported modules, utilities, and available methods.</p>
    </div>
    <button @click="selectPage('API Reference')">API Reference <span>→</span></button>
  </section>
</div>
<div v-else-if="activePage === 'Security Principles'" class="security-principles-page">

  <section id="security-overview" class="security-section security-intro">
    <span class="security-eyebrow">SECURITY FOUNDATION</span>
    <h1>Security Principles</h1>
    <p class="security-lead">
      AVSS Lite provides modular security utilities designed to help developers
      implement essential application security protections.
    </p>

    <div class="security-overview-card">
      <div class="security-overview-mark">A</div>
      <div>
        <strong>Security by design.</strong>
        <p>
          Each security module has a focused responsibility and can be configured
          through the SecurityFramework configuration interface.
        </p>
        <span>CSRF · XSS · Headers · CORS · Cookies · Proxy · Rate Limiting</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security-model" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>01 / SECURITY MODEL</span>
      <h2>Modular Security Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        AVSS Lite organizes its security capabilities into independent modules
        exposed through a single SecurityFramework instance.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-code">
      <div class="security-code-header"><span></span><span></span><span></span><small>security.ts</small></div>
      <pre><code>import { createSecurity } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const security = createSecurity();

// Available security modules
security.csrf;
security.xss;
security.headers;
security.cors;
security.cookies;
security.proxy;
security.rateLimit;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="security-note">
      The module list reflects the public properties exposed by SecurityFramework.
      Each module provides its own methods and configuration.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="security-csrf" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>02 / REQUEST PROTECTION</span>
      <h2>CSRF Protection</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-Site Request Forgery protection provides token generation,
        token validation, and middleware functionality.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-detail-grid">
      <article class="security-detail-card">
        <span class="security-card-index">01</span>
        <h3>Token Generation</h3>
        <p>Generate a CSRF token through the framework security interface.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="security-detail-card">
        <span class="security-card-index">02</span>
        <h3>Token Validation</h3>
        <p>Validate CSRF tokens using the exposed validation method.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="security-detail-card">
        <span class="security-card-index">03</span>
        <h3>Middleware</h3>
        <p>Access the CSRF middleware implementation exposed by the module.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="security-code">
      <div class="security-code-header"><span></span><span></span><span></span><small>csrf.ts</small></div>
      <pre><code>const security = createSecurity();

const token = security.csrf.generateToken();

const isValid = security.csrf.validateToken(token, token);

console.log({ token, isValid });</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security-xss" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>03 / INPUT SANITIZATION</span>
      <h2>XSS Sanitization</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-Site Scripting protection exposes a sanitization function
        for processing HTML input.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-code">
      <div class="security-code-header"><span></span><span></span><span></span><small>xss.ts</small></div>
      <pre><code>const security = createSecurity();

const untrustedInput = '&lt;script&gt;alert("test")&lt;/script&gt;';

const sanitized = security.xss.sanitize(untrustedInput);

console.log(sanitized);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="security-note">
      Sanitization output depends on the implementation and configuration
      of the XSS module. Always apply appropriate output handling in your application.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="security-headers" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>04 / RESPONSE PROTECTION</span>
      <h2>Security Headers</h2>
      <p>
        The headers module provides a builder initialized with the framework's
        default security header configuration.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-feature-card">
      <div class="security-feature-symbol">H</div>
      <div>
        <h3>SecurityHeadersBuilder</h3>
        <p>
          Access the builder through <code>security.headers.builder()</code>.
          The builder starts with <code>withDefaults()</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="security-code">
      <div class="security-code-header"><span></span><span></span><span></span><small>headers.ts</small></div>
      <pre><code>const security = createSecurity();

const headers = security.headers.builder();</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security-cors" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>05 / CROSS-ORIGIN SECURITY</span>
      <h2>CORS</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-Origin Resource Sharing support is provided through the CORS
        module and its header creation utility.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-feature-card">
      <div class="security-feature-symbol">C</div>
      <div>
        <h3>CORS Header Utility</h3>
        <p>
          The framework exposes <code>security.cors.createHeaders</code>
          for creating CORS response headers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security-cookies" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>06 / SESSION DATA</span>
      <h2>Cookie Handling</h2>
      <p>
        The cookie module exposes utilities for parsing cookie header values
        and serializing cookie data.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-detail-grid">
      <article class="security-detail-card">
        <span class="security-card-index">PARSE</span>
        <h3>Cookie Parsing</h3>
        <p>Available through <code>security.cookies.parse</code>.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="security-detail-card">
        <span class="security-card-index">SERIALIZE</span>
        <h3>Cookie Serialization</h3>
        <p>Available through <code>security.cookies.serialize</code>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security-proxy" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>07 / NETWORK TRUST</span>
      <h2>Trusted Proxy</h2>
      <p>
        Proxy utilities provide access to proxy information and a trusted-proxy
        verification function.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-detail-grid">
      <article class="security-detail-card">
        <span class="security-card-index">INFO</span>
        <h3>Proxy Information</h3>
        <p>Exposed through <code>security.proxy.info</code>.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="security-detail-card">
        <span class="security-card-index">TRUST</span>
        <h3>Trusted Proxy Check</h3>
        <p>Exposed through <code>security.proxy.trusted</code>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security-rate-limit" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>08 / REQUEST CONTROL</span>
      <h2>Rate Limiting</h2>
      <p>
        The rate-limit module exposes a check function and includes
        a memory-based storage implementation.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-code">
      <div class="security-code-header"><span></span><span></span><span></span><small>rate-limit.ts</small></div>
      <pre><code>const security = createSecurity();

const result = security.rateLimit.check('127.0.0.1');

console.log(result);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="security-note">
      Rate-limit behavior is controlled by its configuration.
      The example uses the check method exposed by SecurityFramework.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="security-configuration" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>09 / CONFIGURATION</span>
      <h2>Security Configuration</h2>
      <p>
        SecurityFramework accepts an optional SecurityConfig object.
        Its configuration supports individual module settings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-module-grid">
      <article><strong>csrf</strong><span>CSRF configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>xss</strong><span>XSS configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>headers</strong><span>Security headers configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>cors</strong><span>CORS configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>cookies</strong><span>Cookie configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>proxy</strong><span>Proxy configuration</span></article>
      <article><strong>rateLimit</strong><span>Rate limit configuration</span></article>
    </div>

    <div class="security-code">
      <div class="security-code-header"><span></span><span></span><span></span><small>security-config.ts</small></div>
      <pre><code>import { createSecurity } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const security = createSecurity({
  csrf: {},
  xss: {},
  headers: {},
  cors: {},
  cookies: {},
  proxy: {},
  rateLimit: {},
});</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="security-note">
      The empty objects illustrate the available configuration structure.
      Refer to each module's configuration type for supported options.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="security-best-practices" class="security-section">
    <div class="security-heading">
      <span>10 / IMPLEMENTATION GUIDANCE</span>
      <h2>Security Best Practices</h2>
      <p>Use the security modules as part of a broader application security strategy.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="security-practice-list">
      <div><span>01</span><p><strong>Validate untrusted input.</strong> Process incoming data before using it in application logic.</p></div>
      <div><span>02</span><p><strong>Protect state-changing requests.</strong> Apply CSRF protection where the application authentication model requires it.</p></div>
      <div><span>03</span><p><strong>Sanitize HTML input.</strong> Handle untrusted HTML carefully before rendering it.</p></div>
      <div><span>04</span><p><strong>Configure cross-origin access.</strong> Define CORS behavior according to the application's origin requirements.</p></div>
      <div><span>05</span><p><strong>Configure trusted proxies carefully.</strong> Only trust proxy infrastructure controlled by your deployment.</p></div>
      <div><span>06</span><p><strong>Apply request limits.</strong> Configure rate limiting according to endpoint sensitivity and traffic patterns.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security-next" class="security-section security-next">
    <span class="security-eyebrow">NEXT STEP</span>
    <h2>Explore the API Reference.</h2>
    <p>Review the available AVSS Lite exports, modules, and utilities.</p>
    <button class="security-next-button" @click="selectPage('API Reference')">
      Explore API Reference <span>→</span>
    </button>
  </section>

</div>
<div v-else-if="activePage === 'Validation'" class="validation-page">
  <!-- AVSS VALIDATION DOCUMENTATION -->
  <section id="validation-overview" class="validation-section">
    <span class="validation-eyebrow">INPUT VALIDATION</span>
    <h1>Validation</h1>
    <p class="validation-lead">
      Validate application input with schema-based validation powered by Zod.
      AVSS Lite provides a consistent result interface for handling valid and invalid data.
    </p>
    <div class="validation-highlight">
      <strong>Two validation approaches</strong>
      <span>validate() · validateOrThrow()</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="validation-api" class="validation-section">
    <span class="validation-index">01 / PUBLIC API</span>
    <h2>Validation Functions</h2>
    <p>
      The validation module exports two functions. Both accept a Zod schema
      and an unknown input value.
    </p>
    <div class="validation-card-grid">
      <article class="validation-card">
        <h3>validate()</h3>
        <p>Uses safeParse() and returns a ValidationResult instead of throwing for validation failures.</p>
        <code>ValidationResult&lt;T&gt;</code>
      </article>
      <article class="validation-card">
        <h3>validateOrThrow()</h3>
        <p>Uses schema.parse() and returns validated data. Zod throws when the input is invalid.</p>
        <code>T</code>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="validation-result" class="validation-section">
    <span class="validation-index">02 / RESULT STRUCTURE</span>
    <h2>ValidationResult</h2>
    <p>
      The validate() function returns an object containing a success flag.
      Valid input includes data; invalid input includes an array of error messages.
    </p>
    <div class="validation-code">
      <small>validation-result.ts</small>
      <pre><code>export interface ValidationResult&lt;T&gt; {
  success: boolean;
  data?: T;
  errors?: string[];
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="validation-note">
      <strong>Important</strong>
      <p>
        data and errors are optional properties. Check success before accessing
        the corresponding result property.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="validation-example" class="validation-section">
    <span class="validation-index">03 / IMPLEMENTATION</span>
    <h2>Validate an Input Schema</h2>
    <p>
      This example defines a user schema and validates an unknown input.
      The schema rules are provided by Zod.
    </p>
    <div class="validation-code">
      <small>validate.ts</small>
      <pre><code>import { z } from 'zod';
import { validate } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const userSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(2),
  email: z.string().email(),
});

const input: unknown = {
  name: 'Rakha',
  email: 'rakha@example.com',
};

const result = validate(userSchema, input);

if (result.success) {
  console.log(result.data);
} else {
  console.error(result.errors);
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="validation-throw" class="validation-section">
    <span class="validation-index">04 / EXCEPTION-BASED VALIDATION</span>
    <h2>Validate or Throw</h2>
    <p>
      Use validateOrThrow() when invalid input should interrupt the current
      execution flow through a Zod validation exception.
    </p>
    <div class="validation-code">
      <small>validate-or-throw.ts</small>
      <pre><code>import { z } from 'zod';
import { validateOrThrow } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const userSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(2),
  email: z.string().email(),
});

const user = validateOrThrow(userSchema, {
  name: 'Rakha',
  email: 'rakha@example.com',
});

console.log(user);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="validation-note">
      <strong>Handle exceptions</strong>
      <p>
        When using validateOrThrow(), catch validation exceptions at an
        appropriate application boundary if your application needs custom error handling.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="validation-guidelines" class="validation-section">
    <span class="validation-index">05 / BEST PRACTICES</span>
    <h2>Validation Guidelines</h2>
    <ul class="validation-guideline-list">
      <li>Define reusable schemas for application input.</li>
      <li>Treat external input as unknown until it has been validated.</li>
      <li>Check result.success before accessing result.data or result.errors.</li>
      <li>Use validate() when you want to handle failures through a result object.</li>
      <li>Use validateOrThrow() when exception-based control flow is appropriate.</li>
      <li>Do not treat input validation as a replacement for authorization or business rules.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="validation-next" class="validation-section validation-next">
    <span class="validation-eyebrow">NEXT STEP</span>
    <h2>Explore practical examples.</h2>
    <p>See how AVSS Lite modules can be used together in application code.</p>
    <button @click="selectPage('Examples')">Explore Examples →</button>
  </section>
</div>
<div v-else-if="activePage === 'Examples'" class="examples-page">
  <!-- AVSS EXAMPLES DOCUMENTATION -->
  <section id="examples-overview" class="examples-section">
    <span class="examples-eyebrow">PRACTICAL IMPLEMENTATION</span>
    <h1>Examples</h1>
    <p class="examples-lead">
      Practical TypeScript examples using the public AVSS Lite package exports.
      Each example demonstrates an existing API from the toolkit.
    </p>
    <div class="examples-warning">
      <strong>Before you begin</strong>
      <p>
        Install AVSS Lite and its required peer dependencies in your project.
        Examples are starting points; adapt error handling and configuration to your application.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="examples-validation" class="examples-section">
    <span class="examples-index">01 / VALIDATION</span>
    <h2>Validate User Input</h2>
    <p>Combine Zod schemas with AVSS Lite validation utilities.</p>
    <div class="examples-code">
      <small>user-validation.ts</small>
      <pre><code>import { z } from 'zod';
import { validate } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const userSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(2),
  email: z.string().email(),
});

const result = validate(userSchema, {
  name: 'Rakha',
  email: 'rakha@example.com',
});

if (result.success) {
  console.log('Validated:', result.data);
} else {
  console.error('Validation errors:', result.errors);
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="examples-security" class="examples-section">
    <span class="examples-index">02 / SECURITY</span>
    <h2>Generate and Validate a CSRF Token</h2>
    <p>Use the CSRF methods exposed by the SecurityFramework instance.</p>
    <div class="examples-code">
      <small>csrf-example.ts</small>
      <pre><code>import { createSecurity } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const security = createSecurity();

const token = security.csrf.generateToken();
const isValid = security.csrf.validateToken(token, token);

console.log({ token, isValid });</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="examples-note">
      This demonstrates the exposed token methods. A production application
      must obtain and validate tokens through its actual request and session flow.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples-errors" class="examples-section">
    <span class="examples-index">03 / ERROR HANDLING</span>
    <h2>Normalize an AVSS Lite Error</h2>
    <p>Use a built-in error class with the centralized errorHandler.</p>
    <div class="examples-code">
      <small>error-example.ts</small>
      <pre><code>import {
  errorHandler,
  UnauthorizedError,
} from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const error = new UnauthorizedError(
  'Authentication required'
);

const response = errorHandler(error);

console.log(response);</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="examples-logger" class="examples-section">
    <span class="examples-index">04 / LOGGER</span>
    <h2>Application Logging</h2>
    <p>Use the exported logger instance for categorized application messages.</p>
    <div class="examples-code">
      <small>logger-example.ts</small>
      <pre><code>import { logger } from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

logger.info('Application started');
logger.success('Operation completed');
logger.security('Security event detected');
logger.database('Database operation completed');
logger.error('An application error occurred');</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="examples-composition" class="examples-section">
    <span class="examples-index">05 / MODULE COMPOSITION</span>
    <h2>Use Multiple Modules</h2>
    <p>
      The package root exports validation, security, errors, and logger modules.
      Import only the utilities your application needs.
    </p>
    <div class="examples-code">
      <small>application.ts</small>
      <pre><code>import {
  validate,
  createSecurity,
  errorHandler,
  logger,
} from 'avss-lite-Raka503';

const security = createSecurity();

logger.info('Preparing application modules');

const csrfToken = security.csrf.generateToken();

console.log('CSRF token generated:', Boolean(csrfToken));

// Use validate() with your application schema.
// Use errorHandler() at your application error boundary.</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="examples-note">
      These modules are utilities, not a complete authentication provider or
      framework-specific adapter. Integrate them into your application's own lifecycle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples-next" class="examples-section examples-next">
    <span class="examples-eyebrow">NEXT STEP</span>
    <h2>Review the release information.</h2>
    <p>Check the current package version and published changelog status.</p>
    <button @click="selectPage('Changelog')">View Changelog →</button>
  </section>
